  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Nature and Construction
    
    ☽'numu' Witness is not a blade. The name is a deliberate misdirection — the kind Nightshelf keepers favor when outsiders ask questions they should not be answering. The object is a grimoire, small enough to fit in a flipper, heavy as stone. Its covers are volcanic glass layered with ancestor-bone carved into sigils that resist direct observation. The pages beneath are written in luminous ink — pale blue-green, the color of bioluminescence — that reveals itself only in perfect darkness and dies the instant light returns, leaving the book inert. This property ensures that most of the grimoire's contents have never been read by anyone in the Aurora Stack, a restriction the Nightshelf appear to consider a feature rather than a flaw.
    
    The tome was created during the Clever Ice Age, an era whose artifacts I have learned to approach with particular caution. It is central to no fewer than five Nightshelf governing instruments — the Foedus∴vincu, the Pactum∴silenti, the Way∴al, the Firekeeper∴Doctrine, and the Vestigium∴moria — which suggests either extraordinary juridical significance or extraordinary juridical confusion. Both the Weal' Clique~ and The Cell, a Nightshelf merchant guild that rose to dominance during the Orca Incursion, have claimed ownership at various points. The Pactum∴silenti, the Nightshelf silence-law governing artifact transit, controls knowledge of the Witness's movements through the deep tunnels; at least one trader has broken that silence and exposed its routes.
    
    ## Known Contents
    
    Among its legible pages, the grimoire contains a map of corruption thresholds leading to ☽'moth Silvesiris~, a geothermal spring in the Nightshelf's southern reaches, each threshold marked with sigils for *passage* and *price*. It also contains a drawing of the Twilight-bells inscribed: *what rings between fire and ice cannot be owned, only carried, only borne*.
    
    ## Historical Transit
    
    The spine was fractured — a clean break — the night the Weal' Clique~ carried it through the Bra∴conflictu, a three-way conflagration at ☽'slew Ravium~, rather than surrender it to the orca-sworn. During the Zen∴ctum, the tome received ritual scarification across its cover: marks resembling frost-burn that feel warm to the touch. The Nightshelf claim the book chose its own custodians during the Orca Incursion, deciding who would carry it and who would fall.
    
    ### Chain of Custody (Post-Incursion)
    
    | Keeper | Outcome |
    |---|---|
    | First (unnamed) | Drowned in the thermal vents |
    | Second (unnamed) | Entered the deep tunnels; never returned |
    | Keeper Vex of the Weal' Clique~ | Carried the tome before passing it onward |
    | Sivruta~, void-mage | Used it to navigate the deep tunnels to ☽'moth Silvesiris~ |
    | Fissure-Dealer ☽'Sanctteinvaus | Carried it to Wane☽ Oncea; bore the cost of the cleansing |
    
    The pattern deserves attention. Three keepers lost to madness, silence, or death before the tome reached anyone capable of using it. Whether this constitutes selection or attrition depends on one's tolerance for attributing intent to objects.
    
    ## The Cleansing at Wane☽ Oncea
    
    Fissure-Dealer ☽'Sanctteinvaus, a merchant who believed the journey was her own choice, carried the grimoire to the corrupted trade hub Wane☽ Oncea. There it drew simultaneously from its three bound sources — volcanic glass channeling fire, ancestor-bone channeling depth, luminous ink channeling the aurora's absence — and burned the corruption from the settlement's walls. The spine fracture sealed itself afterward, the volcanic glass closing as though it remembered how to be whole. The cost fell on ☽'Sanctteinvaus: her flipper froze permanently in the shape of gripping the tome's weight.
    
    The grimoire rests now in Wane☽ Oncea's uppermost chamber.
    
    ## Agency
    
    The tome's influence operates below the threshold of decision. Bearers report contracts appearing overnight in locked stalls, route entries written in another merchant's cipher by no merchant's flipper, travel logs rearranging themselves to indicate the tome's destination. Those who carry it dream of patient observation — something that already knows where they are going. Whether this constitutes the accumulated will of every ancestor whose bone is in its cover, or something older, the sources do not agree. The ice remembers what ☽'numu' Witness has seen. So, by all accounts, does anyone foolish enough to read it.
